# Controls what reaches the Docker daemon as build context — defense in
# depth against .env ever reaching the daemon at all, and meaningfully
# faster builds (state/ alone carries tens of MB of research data).
#
# Does NOT exclude engine/, backtest/, tests/, or deploy/ — three
# Dockerfiles share this one build context (deploy/docker-compose.yml sets
# `context: ..` for all of them). dashboard/Dockerfile and
# mcp_server/Dockerfile each do a narrow, explicit COPY (e.g.
# `COPY engine/__init__.py engine/attribution.py engine/config.py`) that
# is itself the trust boundary — never `COPY . .` — so those two images
# only ever contain the files their own Dockerfile names, regardless of
# what's present in the context. deploy/engine.Dockerfile is the trusted
# tier and legitimately COPYs all four directories (scripts.
# iwm_breakout_forward, a cron job, imports backtest.intraday; tests/ ships
# so deploy/upgrade.sh can run the suite inside a candidate image). This
# file controls what's SENT to the daemon; each Dockerfile's own COPY
# instructions control what actually lands IN that image — the two are
# complementary, not redundant, and state/reports/logs/.env stay excluded
# here because no image should ever bake them in at all (they're runtime
# volumes or, for .env, never baked in anywhere).

.git/
.venv/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.env
.env.example
state/
reports/
exports/
logs/
.claude/
